<h2>What a caveat is</h2>
<p>Section 148A of the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908 permits a caveat to be lodged where an application <em>is expected to be made, or has been made</em>, in a suit or proceeding that is instituted or about to be instituted. The caveat is not a pleading and raises no claim of its own. It is a formal intimation to the court: if a particular application comes, the caveator claims the right to appear and asks to be heard before any order is made on it. In Delhi practice the caveat is most familiar as the decree-holder’s shield against an ex parte stay in an anticipated appeal, but the text of the provision is wider and covers any expected application in a civil proceeding.</p>

<h2>Who may lodge one</h2>
<p>The provision opens the remedy to <strong>any person claiming a right to appear before the court</strong> on the hearing of the expected application. That naturally includes the party who succeeded in the court below — the plaintiff whose suit was decreed, the defendant whose objections prevailed, the party in whose favour an interim arrangement stands. It can also include a person who was not a party below but whose interests the anticipated application would directly touch, provided a genuine right to appear can be asserted. What the section does not contemplate is a caveat by a stranger with no stake in the expected proceeding.</p>

<h2>The statutory mechanics</h2>
<p>Section 148A works through four interlocking duties. Once the caveat is lodged, the caveator must serve notice of the caveat by registered post on the person expected to make the application. Thereafter, if the application is in fact filed, the <strong>court</strong> must serve notice of the application on the caveator. The <strong>applicant</strong>, in turn, must furnish the caveator, at the caveator’s expense, with a copy of the application and of the papers and documents filed or relied upon in support of it. The combined effect is the caveat’s real value: the application cannot proceed to an interim order behind the caveator’s back, and the caveator argues from the same papers the applicant has placed before the court.</p>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><h4>Life: 90 days</h4><p>Under Section 148A(5) the caveat remains in force for ninety days from lodging; it then lapses unless the application was made before expiry.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Right: notice + hearing</h4><p>The caveator is entitled to notice of the application and to be heard before any interim order is passed on it.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><h4>Duty: papers to caveator</h4><p>The applicant must supply the application and supporting documents to the caveator at the caveator’s expense.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Where caveats are filed in Delhi</h2>
<p>The caveat belongs in the court where the application is anticipated. For a money or property decree of a district court in Delhi, the expected challenge is commonly a regular first appeal before the Delhi High Court, often accompanied by a stay application; the caveat is therefore lodged on the appellate side of the High Court. Orders of the kinds appealable under Order XLIII attract first appeals from orders, and judgments of a Single Judge on the original side may be anticipated to travel to a Division Bench in a Letters Patent Appeal — in each situation the caveat is framed for the forum and the proceeding expected, described as precisely as the caveator can manage. Where the anticipated application lies within the district judiciary itself — an appeal to the District Judge, or an application in a pending suit — the caveat is lodged in the concerned district court. Filing in both tiers follows the registry’s prescribed form and fees, including electronic filing where the rules of the court provide for it.</p>

<h2>What a caveat cannot do</h2>
<p>The limits are as important as the entitlement. A caveat does not prevent the opposite party from filing the application, does not stall its registration or numbering, and does not decide or even influence the merits. It creates no stay and vacates none. If, despite the caveat, an interim order comes to be passed without notice to the caveator, the order is procedurally vulnerable and the caveator’s remedy is to bring the lapse to the court’s notice promptly and seek a hearing. And because Section 148A is part of the civil procedure code, the general rule is that caveats have no place in criminal proceedings, which are governed by their own code containing no equivalent general provision.</p>

<h2>Drafting essentials</h2>
<div class="check"><ul>
<li>Cause title naming the court and describing the anticipated proceeding — appeal, application or petition — as specifically as possible</li>
<li>Particulars of the decree or order expected to be challenged: court, case number, date and parties</li>
<li>The caveator’s right to appear, stated in a sentence — typically as the party in whose favour the decree or order stands</li>
<li>A prayer that no interim order be passed without prior notice to the caveator and an opportunity of hearing</li>
<li>Full address for service, including counsel’s particulars, e-mail and telephone, so notice actually reaches the caveator in time</li>
<li>Vakalatnama of the advocate lodging the caveat, with the court fee the registry prescribes</li>
<li>Proof of despatch of the caveat notice by registered post to the expected applicant, kept on record</li>
<li>A diary entry for the ninetieth day, so a fresh caveat is lodged if the risk outlives the first one</li>
</ul></div>

<div class="note"><p>A caveat costs little and asks for nothing except a hearing — which is precisely why it should be lodged as a matter of course the day a favourable decree or order is pronounced, and renewed every ninety days until the opposite party’s limitation for challenge has safely run out.</p></div>
